rent, which might be the container? Or might be an intermediate element.\n\n Instead, we can compute the scrollTop like this:\n\n ```\n var rectEl = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n var rectContainer = container.getBoundingClientRect();\n // The current offset from the top of the container's visible area is rectEl.top - rectContainer.top.\n // We want the top of the element to be at 10px below the top of the container. So we need:\n var desiredScrollTop = container.scrollTop + (rectEl.top - rectContainer.top) - 10;\n container.scrollTop = desiredScrollTop;\n ```\n\n But note: this would require the container to have its current scroll position, and we are adjusting by the difference.\n\n Alternatively, we can avoid that and set the scrollTop based on the element's position relative to the container without using client rects:\n\n Since the container's scrollTop is the amount hidden above, to reveal the element at a position 10px below the top, we should set:\n\n container.scrollTop = el.offsetTop - 10;\n\n But this assumes that the container has no borders and that the element's offsetTop is relative to the container. Which is true only if the container is the offset parent.\n\n How about we try a simpler method: we call scrollIntoView and then adjust the scroll position by 10px upwards? Wait, no: because we are too high by 10px, we want to scroll down by 10px? Actually, if the element is 10px too high, then we are seeing it 10px above the top? Then we need to move the container's scrollTop down by 10px? That would move the content upwards, bringing the element 10px lower? No, because:\n\n If the scrollTop increases, the content moves downward (so the element moves upward in the view). If we decrease scrollTop, the content moves upward (so the element moves downward in the view).\n\n Explanation:\n\n When we have a scrollTop value of 0, the top of the content is at the top of the container. If we set scrollTop to 10, then the content is scrolled down by 10 pixels, meaning the top of the content is 10px above the top of the container (so we are seeing from 10px below the top of the content).\n\n Therefore, to bring the element down by 10px in the container, we want to reduce the scrollTop by 10px? Actually, that moves the element upwards? \n\n Let me think: if we set scrollTop = current - 10, then the content is moved upward by 10px, so the element that was at the top (already visible) moves down? Yes: because we are showing 10px of previously hidden content at the top, so the entire content moves downward relative to the container by 10px? \n\n Actually: reducing the scrollTop moves the content UP, meaning the element that was at the top will now be 10px lower in the container? No, wait:\n\n Suppose we have an element that is exactly at the top of the container. Then scrollTop is set to the element's offsetTop. If we reduce scrollTop by 10, then we are moving the content upward, which means the element now appears 10px lower? Yes, because we pulled the content above the element into the view? \n\n But of course, the element's position in the container's coordinate system doesn't change, but the part of the content that is visible changes.\n\n Actually, let me clarify:\n\n We want the element to be positioned 10px below the top. After scrollIntoView(true), the element is at the top. To achieve 10px below, we want to scroll the container additional 10px downward? How?\n\n If we set `container.scrollTop = container.scrollTop + 10`, then we move the content upward? No: increasing scrollTop scrolls the content downward (i.e., hides more top content and shows more bottom content). But note:\n\n Initially, scrollIntoView(true) sets the container's scrollTop to the value that puts the element at the top. Then, we add 10 pixels to scrollTop: that would move the element upward? Because the container now shows content that is 10px above the element? \n\n We want the element to go down by 10px. So we need to show 10px of content above the el