d? We want exactly the ones under `/usr/local/texlive/`.\n\n Actually, we can use:\n\n   find . -type l -lname '/usr/local/texlive/*' -delete\n\n However, note that the target might have variations. And the pattern must match exactly? Actually, the pattern in `-lname` is a shell-style pattern and `*` matches any string. So `/usr/local/texlive/*` will match any symlink whose target starts with `/usr/local/texlive/`? Yes, because the pattern must match the entire string. However, if there are symlinks that have targets exactly starting with that path, it will match.\n\n But caution: what about relative symlinks? The example shows absolute symlinks.\n\n The user says they are in `/usr/local/bin` and the symlinks are placed there. So the links are absolute? They are absolute in the example.\n\n So we can do:\n   find . -maxdepth 1 -type l -lname '/usr/local/texlive/*' -delete\n\n Why `.`? Because the user is in `/usr/local/bin`. We can also specify the directory:\n\n   find /usr/local/bin -maxdepth 1 -type l -lname '/usr/local/texlive/*' -delete\n\n But note: the user is in that directory? We can use relative paths.\n\n Alternatively, we can use `-exec` and `readlink` for more control:\n\n   find . -maxdepth 1 -type l -exec sh -c 'for link; do if readlink \"$link\" | grep -q \"^/usr/local/texlive/\"; then rm \"$link\"; fi; done' _ {} +\n\n Explanation:\n   - `-maxdepth 1` to only look in current directory.\n   - We pass the found files to a shell that loops over each, checks if the symlink target starts with `/usr/local/texlive/` (using `grep -q` and `^` to anchor) then removes the symlink.\n\n This avoids matching if the string appears elsewhere in the path? Only at the beginning.\n\n But note: the example shows absolute paths. There might be relative symlinks? The user doesn't mention, so we assume absolute? But to be safe, we can match absolute only.\n\n However, the user's example links are absolute. So either method might work.\n\n Another tool: `symlinks` (if inst