nce would look like:\n\n- Put disc 1 on the changer with Side 1 facing up.\n- Then put disc 2 on top of disc 1 with Side 2 facing up.\n\nThe turntable would play Side 1 (on disc 1) first, then Side 2 (on disc 2). Then you'd have to flip the stack? But flipping a stack was not automatic. So after playing Side 2 (on disc 2), you would have to manually flip the stack to play Side 3 (on disc 2) and then Side 4 (on disc 1). This is inefficient because you have to flip the entire stack.\n\nAlternatively, if you arrange the sides:\n\nDisc 1: Side 1 and Side 4\nDisc 2: Side 2 and Side 3\n\nNow, when you stack:\n\nFirst, put disc 1 (with Side 1 up) at the bottom. Then put disc 2 (with Side 2 up) on top.\n\nThe turntable plays Side 2 (disc 2) first? Then Side 1 (disc 1) because disc 2 was on top? But that isn't the order.\n\nAlternatively, you can put disc 1 (with Side 1 up) on the bottom and disc 2 (with Side 3 up) on top? Then you play Side 3? That isn't the start.\n\nSo the trick is: the listener would not necessarily stack both records at once. Instead, they might:\n\n1. Start by playing disc 1: Side 1. Then flip disc 1 to play Side 4? But that would break the sequence because we haven't played Side 2/3.\n\nAlternatively, an automatic changer that could flip? Such changers existed but were less common.\n\nActually, the reason for the numbering is that the records were intended to be played without a changer. The listener would:\n\n- Play disc 1, Side 1 (movements 1).\n- Then play disc 2, Side 2 (movements 2).\n- Then flip disc 2 to Side 3 (movements 3 and start of 4).\n- Then flip disc 2 off and put disc 1 back on to play Side 4 (conclusion).\n\nThis required two disc changes: after Side1, change to disc2; after Side3, change back to disc1.\n\nBut they called it \"automatic sequence\"? Actually, this labeling enabled a different method: using a record changer that held two records. By putting disc 1 on the bottom and disc 2 on top (with Side 1 facing up on disc 1 and Side